From: SPIranha <https://github.com/MTSistemi/SPIranha>
Subject: [PATCH] report a firmware version in the programmer name

A board gives the host no way to say which firmware it is running. The flash
holds no version, the USB serial number is the chip's unique id, and the
descriptors are the same across builds. So a host tool cannot know whether the
programmer in front of it predates a fix.

The serprog name is a fixed 16-byte field and the host reads all sixteen, so a
version fits in it without a terminator and without a new command. Hosts that
do not care print it verbatim, which is what flashrom already does:

    Programmer name is "pico-serprog1.1"

1.1 is the first build that says so; anything reporting a bare "pico-serprog"
is 1.0 or older, which is exactly the distinction the host needs.
---
diff --git a/main.c b/main.c
--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -33,7 +33,10 @@ uint cs_pin = SPI_CS_0;

 uint baud = 12000000; /* Default to 12MHz */

-static const char progname[16] = "pico-serprog";
+/* The name is a 16-byte field and the host reads all 16 of them, so a version
+ * fits without terminator. It is the only way for the host to tell which
+ * firmware a board is running: the board reports it, nothing else does. */
+static const char progname[16] = "pico-serprog1.1";

 /* Map of supported serprog commands */
 static const uint32_t cmdmap[8] = {
